1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,670
One hundred and sixty-six billion dollars.

2
00:00:03,670 --> 00:00:05,751
That is roughly how much the United

3
00:00:05,751 --> 00:00:07,832
States had charged in tariffs that the

4
00:00:07,832 --> 00:00:10,121
Supreme Court later said the President had

5
00:00:10,121 --> 00:00:11,970
no power to impose.

6
00:00:12,160 --> 00:00:14,293
The number is the customs agency's own

7
00:00:14,293 --> 00:00:15,475
estimate.

8
00:00:15,717 --> 00:00:18,072
Now the government is paying that money

9
00:00:18,072 --> 00:00:18,907
back.

10
00:00:19,097 --> 00:00:20,353
By late October,

11
00:00:20,353 --> 00:00:22,269
it had sent about one hundred and

12
00:00:22,269 --> 00:00:24,611
twenty-seven point seven billion dollars

13
00:00:24,611 --> 00:00:25,441
in refunds,

14
00:00:25,441 --> 00:00:26,484
with interest,

15
00:00:26,484 --> 00:00:28,787
to the Treasury for payment.

16
00:00:28,897 --> 00:00:30,606
But almost none of it is going

17
00:00:30,606 --> 00:00:32,741
to the people who paid higher prices

18
00:00:32,741 --> 00:00:33,982
in the store.

19
00:00:34,217 --> 00:00:37,185
So who really paid those tariffs,

20
00:00:37,185 --> 00:00:40,786
and who is getting the money back?

21
00:00:41,050 --> 00:00:42,970
By the end of this video,

22
00:00:42,970 --> 00:00:45,158
you will know how a tariff travels

23
00:00:45,158 --> 00:00:47,322
from a port to your shopping cart,

24
00:00:47,322 --> 00:00:49,242
how the refund machine works,

25
00:00:49,242 --> 00:00:50,944
and why the checks are going to

26
00:00:50,944 --> 00:00:51,892
companies,

27
00:00:51,892 --> 00:00:53,221
not to you.

28
00:00:58,883 --> 00:01:01,835
The tariffs were imposed under an

29
00:01:01,835 --> 00:01:05,683
emergency powers law known as IEEPA.

30
00:01:05,873 --> 00:01:07,513
Importers sued.

31
00:01:07,533 --> 00:01:09,355
On February twentieth,

32
00:01:09,355 --> 00:01:10,913
twenty twenty-six,

33
00:01:10,913 --> 00:01:13,527
the Supreme Court decided the case,

34
00:01:13,527 --> 00:01:15,877
Learning Resources versus Trump,

35
00:01:15,877 --> 00:01:18,326
by a vote of six to three.

36
00:01:18,583 --> 00:01:21,123
The ruling was short and direct.

37
00:01:21,123 --> 00:01:22,201
In the Court's words,

38
00:01:22,201 --> 00:01:24,842
the emergency law does not authorize the

39
00:01:24,842 --> 00:01:27,203
President to impose tariffs.

40
00:01:27,303 --> 00:01:28,843
Chief Justice Roberts wrote for the

41
00:01:28,843 --> 00:01:30,213
majority.

42
00:01:30,253 --> 00:01:31,535
Justices Thomas,

43
00:01:31,535 --> 00:01:34,307
Alito and Kavanaugh dissented.

44
00:01:34,550 --> 00:01:37,850
But the decision left one huge question

45
00:01:37,850 --> 00:01:39,110
open.

46
00:01:39,350 --> 00:01:41,626
Justice Kavanaugh pointed it out in his

47
00:01:41,626 --> 00:01:42,640
dissent.

48
00:01:42,900 --> 00:01:43,550
The Court,

49
00:01:43,550 --> 00:01:44,201
he wrote,

50
00:01:44,201 --> 00:01:46,443
said nothing about whether or how the

51
00:01:46,443 --> 00:01:48,686
government should return the billions it

52
00:01:48,686 --> 00:01:51,080
had collected from importers.

53
00:01:51,390 --> 00:01:53,297
He predicted it would likely be a

54
00:01:53,297 --> 00:01:54,070
mess.

55
00:01:54,270 --> 00:01:55,673
And he added a line that sits

56
00:01:55,673 --> 00:01:57,500
at the heart of this story.

57
00:01:57,990 --> 00:02:00,601
Some importers may have already passed on

58
00:02:00,601 --> 00:02:02,507
the costs to consumers.

59
00:02:02,750 --> 00:02:05,150
The scale was enormous.

60
00:02:05,180 --> 00:02:07,450
According to the customs agency's

61
00:02:07,450 --> 00:02:08,335
estimate,

62
00:02:08,335 --> 00:02:10,151
those tariffs had been charged on

63
00:02:10,151 --> 00:02:12,398
fifty-three million import shipments,

64
00:02:12,398 --> 00:02:14,667
paid by about three hundred and thirty

65
00:02:14,667 --> 00:02:17,083
thousand different importers.

66
00:02:17,317 --> 00:02:19,847
Let me draw who pays a tariff.

67
00:02:20,057 --> 00:02:22,197
Here is a factory abroad.

68
00:02:22,347 --> 00:02:25,034
Here is the American company that imports

69
00:02:25,034 --> 00:02:26,047
its goods.

70
00:02:26,287 --> 00:02:28,421
The tariff is charged at the port,

71
00:02:28,421 --> 00:02:31,177
and the importer pays it to customs.

72
00:02:31,207 --> 00:02:33,164
Then the goods go to a store,

73
00:02:33,164 --> 00:02:34,507
and to you.

74
00:02:34,597 --> 00:02:37,127
The law sees only the first payment.

75
00:02:37,127 --> 00:02:40,268
Your price tag is a separate story.

76
00:02:40,517 --> 00:02:43,877
So the legal question was settled.

77
00:02:43,877 --> 00:02:46,447
The money question was just beginning.

78
00:02:46,447 --> 00:02:47,583
And the first part of it is

79
00:02:47,583 --> 00:02:48,417
simple.

80
00:02:48,417 --> 00:02:50,222
Who actually paid?

81
00:03:00,883 --> 00:03:02,005
On paper,

82
00:03:02,005 --> 00:03:03,883
the importer pays.

83
00:03:03,883 --> 00:03:06,293
That is how a tariff works.

84
00:03:06,373 --> 00:03:08,521
A company brings a shipment of toys

85
00:03:08,521 --> 00:03:10,218
or shoes into the country,

86
00:03:10,218 --> 00:03:12,345
and before customs releases it,

87
00:03:12,345 --> 00:03:14,663
the company pays the duty.

88
00:03:14,943 --> 00:03:17,798
The government never sends you a bill.

89
00:03:18,050 --> 00:03:20,685
But companies do not have to keep

90
00:03:20,685 --> 00:03:21,870
that cost.

91
00:03:21,940 --> 00:03:24,070
They can raise their prices.

92
00:03:24,070 --> 00:03:26,490
Economists measure this with a number

93
00:03:26,490 --> 00:03:27,970
called pass-through.

94
00:03:28,160 --> 00:03:30,352
Researchers at the Yale Budget Lab

95
00:03:30,352 --> 00:03:32,960
estimate that for everyday core goods,

96
00:03:32,960 --> 00:03:35,371
between forty-six and eighty-six percent

97
00:03:35,371 --> 00:03:37,782
of the tariffs showed up in consumer

98
00:03:37,782 --> 00:03:38,417
prices,

99
00:03:38,417 --> 00:03:40,990
depending on how you measure it.

100
00:03:41,250 --> 00:03:43,355
Researchers at the New York Fed found

101
00:03:43,355 --> 00:03:44,968
something similar,

102
00:03:44,968 --> 00:03:46,270
but slower.

103
00:03:46,330 --> 00:03:48,779
They estimate that nearly ninety percent

104
00:03:48,779 --> 00:03:51,025
of the twenty twenty-five tariffs passed

105
00:03:51,025 --> 00:03:53,870
through into the prices of imported goods.

106
00:03:54,020 --> 00:03:56,196
The effect on store prices built up

107
00:03:56,196 --> 00:03:57,380
more gradually,

108
00:03:57,380 --> 00:03:59,189
over many months.

109
00:03:59,450 --> 00:04:01,942
So the honest answer is that the

110
00:04:01,942 --> 00:04:03,650
cost was shared.

111
00:04:03,720 --> 00:04:06,380
Importers paid customs first.

112
00:04:06,500 --> 00:04:08,597
Then many of them passed a large

113
00:04:08,597 --> 00:04:09,739
part of it on,

114
00:04:09,739 --> 00:04:11,970
to stores and to shoppers.

115
00:04:12,310 --> 00:04:14,771
Exactly how much reached you depends on

116
00:04:14,771 --> 00:04:16,762
the product and the company,

117
00:04:16,762 --> 00:04:19,600
and no one can trace every dollar.

118
00:04:19,850 --> 00:04:22,350
Let me draw a tariff dollar.

119
00:04:22,490 --> 00:04:24,770
One dollar goes to customs.

120
00:04:24,850 --> 00:04:26,446
Part of it stays with the importer

121
00:04:26,446 --> 00:04:28,110
as a lower profit.

122
00:04:28,280 --> 00:04:30,252
Part of it moves down the chain,

123
00:04:30,252 --> 00:04:32,224
into the wholesale price,

124
00:04:32,224 --> 00:04:33,710
then the store price.

125
00:04:33,870 --> 00:04:36,051
By the time it reaches your receipt,

126
00:04:36,051 --> 00:04:38,033
it is hidden inside the price of

127
00:04:38,033 --> 00:04:40,175
a sweater or a toaster.

128
00:04:40,417 --> 00:04:42,977
Now run that chain backwards.

129
00:04:42,977 --> 00:04:44,947
When the money comes back,

130
00:04:44,947 --> 00:04:46,545
where does it land?

131
00:04:52,183 --> 00:04:54,905
The customs agency built a new refund

132
00:04:54,905 --> 00:04:57,733
system inside its import software.

133
00:04:57,823 --> 00:04:59,513
It opened in stages.

134
00:04:59,513 --> 00:04:59,939
First,

135
00:04:59,939 --> 00:05:00,813
in April,

136
00:05:00,813 --> 00:05:02,483
for recent shipments.

137
00:05:02,483 --> 00:05:02,979
Then,

138
00:05:02,979 --> 00:05:03,736
in June,

139
00:05:03,736 --> 00:05:05,903
for more complex cases.

140
00:05:05,903 --> 00:05:07,168
And in October,

141
00:05:07,168 --> 00:05:08,648
for the oldest shipments,

142
00:05:08,648 --> 00:05:10,792
but only for importers that had gone

143
00:05:10,792 --> 00:05:12,893
to court and won an order.

144
00:05:13,150 --> 00:05:15,499
That last step pushed thousands of

145
00:05:15,499 --> 00:05:17,860
companies into court.

146
00:05:18,090 --> 00:05:20,668
More than four thousand cases were filed

147
00:05:20,668 --> 00:05:22,705
at the trade court in New York,

148
00:05:22,705 --> 00:05:25,025
many of them simply to get the

149
00:05:25,025 --> 00:05:27,346
court order that the refund system

150
00:05:27,346 --> 00:05:28,719
requires.

151
00:05:28,983 --> 00:05:31,273
The money moved fast.

152
00:05:31,423 --> 00:05:32,798
By the end of July,

153
00:05:32,798 --> 00:05:34,791
the agency said it had sent about

154
00:05:34,791 --> 00:05:36,963
one hundred billion dollars in refunds,

155
00:05:36,963 --> 00:05:38,339
including interest,

156
00:05:38,339 --> 00:05:40,533
to the Treasury for payment.

157
00:05:40,963 --> 00:05:42,251
By late October,

158
00:05:42,251 --> 00:05:44,215
the total reached one hundred and

159
00:05:44,215 --> 00:05:47,216
twenty-seven point seven billion dollars.

160
00:05:47,483 --> 00:05:49,921
You can see it in the Treasury's

161
00:05:49,921 --> 00:05:51,053
own books.

162
00:05:51,053 --> 00:05:53,485
In June twenty twenty-six alone,

163
00:05:53,485 --> 00:05:55,941
customs refunds were about forty-nine

164
00:05:55,941 --> 00:05:57,523
billion dollars.

165
00:05:57,633 --> 00:05:58,666
For three months,

166
00:05:58,666 --> 00:06:00,228
from May to July,

167
00:06:00,228 --> 00:06:02,082
refunds were so large that the

168
00:06:02,082 --> 00:06:04,995
government's net customs revenue actually

169
00:06:04,995 --> 00:06:06,655
went negative.

170
00:06:06,917 --> 00:06:09,387
And some money is simply waiting.

171
00:06:09,387 --> 00:06:11,662
About about nine hundred and ninety

172
00:06:11,662 --> 00:06:13,295
million dollars was on hold,

173
00:06:13,295 --> 00:06:15,570
because the importers had not yet given

174
00:06:15,570 --> 00:06:17,824
the government their bank details.

175
00:06:18,083 --> 00:06:20,683
Let me draw the refund pipe.

176
00:06:20,693 --> 00:06:21,629
On the left,

177
00:06:21,629 --> 00:06:23,284
the customs agency,

178
00:06:23,284 --> 00:06:25,083
checking each shipment.

179
00:06:25,163 --> 00:06:26,172
In the middle,

180
00:06:26,172 --> 00:06:27,181
the Treasury,

181
00:06:27,181 --> 00:06:28,193
paying out.

182
00:06:28,253 --> 00:06:29,090
On the right,

183
00:06:29,090 --> 00:06:31,183
the importer's bank account.

184
00:06:31,183 --> 00:06:32,723
There is no pipe that leads to

185
00:06:32,723 --> 00:06:33,983
a shopper.

186
00:06:34,113 --> 00:06:35,772
The system was built to find the

187
00:06:35,772 --> 00:06:37,617
company that paid at the port,

188
00:06:37,617 --> 00:06:39,669
and only that company.

189
00:06:39,917 --> 00:06:42,286
So the checks are landing in corporate

190
00:06:42,286 --> 00:06:43,597
bank accounts.

191
00:06:43,607 --> 00:06:45,428
Here is what some of those companies

192
00:06:45,428 --> 00:06:47,040
say they did with them.

193
00:06:52,683 --> 00:06:55,803
The customs agency is blunt about it.

194
00:06:55,833 --> 00:06:57,911
It can only refund the duties to

195
00:06:57,911 --> 00:06:59,552
the importer of record,

196
00:06:59,552 --> 00:07:02,853
the company named on the import paperwork.

197
00:07:03,193 --> 00:07:05,091
There is no government refund for

198
00:07:05,091 --> 00:07:06,323
consumers.

199
00:07:06,503 --> 00:07:08,542
If you paid more for a toaster

200
00:07:08,542 --> 00:07:10,580
or a pair of shoes in twenty

201
00:07:10,580 --> 00:07:11,574
twenty-five,

202
00:07:11,574 --> 00:07:13,358
there is no form you can fill

203
00:07:13,358 --> 00:07:15,142
out to claim your share from the

204
00:07:15,142 --> 00:07:16,506
government.

205
00:07:16,750 --> 00:07:19,550
Look at the retailers' own filings.

206
00:07:19,550 --> 00:07:21,560
Target reported nine hundred and

207
00:07:21,560 --> 00:07:24,016
ninety-four million dollars of tariff

208
00:07:24,016 --> 00:07:25,557
refunds in one quarter,

209
00:07:25,557 --> 00:07:27,790
booked as a reduction in its cost

210
00:07:27,790 --> 00:07:29,060
of sales.

211
00:07:29,090 --> 00:07:30,169
In plain words,

212
00:07:30,169 --> 00:07:32,632
the refund made its costs lower,

213
00:07:32,632 --> 00:07:34,893
and its profit higher.

214
00:07:35,150 --> 00:07:37,470
Costco said it paid about five hundred

215
00:07:37,470 --> 00:07:39,790
million dollars in these tariffs and had

216
00:07:39,790 --> 00:07:41,878
received about three hundred and

217
00:07:41,878 --> 00:07:43,870
thirty-nine million back.

218
00:07:44,270 --> 00:07:45,840
It said part of that money was

219
00:07:45,840 --> 00:07:47,998
being invested in lower prices for its

220
00:07:47,998 --> 00:07:48,990
members.

221
00:07:49,270 --> 00:07:52,072
The clothing maker lululemon received one

222
00:07:52,072 --> 00:07:54,227
hundred and thirty-four point five million

223
00:07:54,227 --> 00:07:54,851
dollars,

224
00:07:54,851 --> 00:07:56,960
which lifted its gross margin.

225
00:07:57,120 --> 00:07:59,146
And the toy maker Mattel said it

226
00:07:59,146 --> 00:08:01,375
received eighty-eight million dollars,

227
00:08:01,375 --> 00:08:03,402
which helped push its gross margin to

228
00:08:03,402 --> 00:08:05,578
almost fifty-five percent.

229
00:08:05,817 --> 00:08:08,977
The most direct case is FedEx.

230
00:08:09,017 --> 00:08:11,285
In its latest quarterly filing,

231
00:08:11,285 --> 00:08:13,805
FedEx said it had received about three

232
00:08:13,805 --> 00:08:16,073
point three billion dollars in refunds,

233
00:08:16,073 --> 00:08:18,822
and recorded about two point seven billion

234
00:08:18,822 --> 00:08:21,113
dollars as money owed back to those

235
00:08:21,113 --> 00:08:22,400
customers.

236
00:08:22,650 --> 00:08:25,066
Let me draw three ways a refund

237
00:08:25,066 --> 00:08:26,200
can go.

238
00:08:26,200 --> 00:08:27,654
Some becomes profit,

239
00:08:27,654 --> 00:08:30,150
and shows up in a better quarter.

240
00:08:30,560 --> 00:08:33,197
Some goes back to business customers,

241
00:08:33,197 --> 00:08:35,440
like the shippers paying their clients.

242
00:08:35,440 --> 00:08:38,460
And some may turn into lower prices.

243
00:08:38,510 --> 00:08:40,384
Which one happens is up to each

244
00:08:40,384 --> 00:08:41,686
company.

245
00:08:41,950 --> 00:08:44,692
Which is exactly why shoppers are now

246
00:08:44,692 --> 00:08:46,114
going to court.

247
00:08:51,750 --> 00:08:54,121
Consumers have filed class actions against

248
00:08:54,121 --> 00:08:56,230
several big companies.

249
00:08:56,320 --> 00:08:58,318
Costco disclosed four of them,

250
00:08:58,318 --> 00:09:00,563
which allege that tariffs were passed on

251
00:09:00,563 --> 00:09:02,583
to members through higher prices and

252
00:09:02,583 --> 00:09:04,530
should now be refunded.

253
00:09:04,610 --> 00:09:06,950
FedEx disclosed fourteen.

254
00:09:06,950 --> 00:09:08,516
These are allegations,

255
00:09:08,516 --> 00:09:10,932
and the cases are still going on.

256
00:09:11,183 --> 00:09:13,167
The one class action a court has

257
00:09:13,167 --> 00:09:16,743
actually certified so far is different.

258
00:09:16,803 --> 00:09:19,268
In October twenty twenty-six,

259
00:09:19,268 --> 00:09:21,509
the trade court certified a class of

260
00:09:21,509 --> 00:09:22,481
importers,

261
00:09:22,481 --> 00:09:24,946
the companies that paid the duties,

262
00:09:24,946 --> 00:09:26,689
so that all of them can claim

263
00:09:26,689 --> 00:09:28,783
their refunds together.

264
00:09:29,050 --> 00:09:30,074
Meanwhile,

265
00:09:30,074 --> 00:09:33,300
the tariffs did not simply disappear.

266
00:09:33,350 --> 00:09:34,903
Days after the ruling,

267
00:09:34,903 --> 00:09:37,581
the President used a different law,

268
00:09:37,581 --> 00:09:39,134
Section one twenty-two,

269
00:09:39,134 --> 00:09:41,385
to impose a ten percent surcharge for

270
00:09:41,385 --> 00:09:43,560
one hundred and fifty days.

271
00:09:43,720 --> 00:09:45,650
When it expired in July,

272
00:09:45,650 --> 00:09:48,251
new tariffs followed under yet another

273
00:09:48,251 --> 00:09:48,663
law,

274
00:09:48,663 --> 00:09:49,942
Section three oh one,

275
00:09:49,942 --> 00:09:52,060
on sixty economies.

276
00:09:52,340 --> 00:09:53,559
The Tax Foundation,

277
00:09:53,559 --> 00:09:54,364
a think tank,

278
00:09:54,364 --> 00:09:57,050
still estimates the tariff burden at about

279
00:09:57,050 --> 00:09:58,909
eight hundred and twenty dollars per

280
00:09:58,909 --> 00:10:02,195
American household in twenty twenty-six.

281
00:10:02,450 --> 00:10:04,630
Let me draw the loop.

282
00:10:04,630 --> 00:10:06,800
Tariffs go in at the port.

283
00:10:06,800 --> 00:10:09,060
Prices rise on the shelf.

284
00:10:09,140 --> 00:10:11,840
The court says the tariffs were illegal.

285
00:10:11,970 --> 00:10:13,207
Refunds come out,

286
00:10:13,207 --> 00:10:15,020
to the importers.

287
00:10:15,020 --> 00:10:17,506
And new tariffs start the loop again,

288
00:10:17,506 --> 00:10:19,362
under a new law.

289
00:10:19,617 --> 00:10:23,127
So who actually gets the tariff refund?

290
00:10:23,167 --> 00:10:25,517
The companies that paid at the border.

291
00:10:25,667 --> 00:10:27,892
